{"id":"v9","level":4,"levelName":"The verb system","n":4,"of":15,"title":"You have to, you can, you mustn't","titleHe":"מִשְׁפָּט סְתָמִי","goal":"Build the sentences that have no subject and no conjugated verb.","sections":[{"type":"text","body":"A large family of everyday Hebrew sentences has no subject at all and no verb in any tense. It is one word — צָרִיךְ, אֶפְשָׁר, אָסוּר — followed by the infinitive you have just learned."},{"type":"table","caption":"The words that carry these sentences","head":["meaning","agrees with a person?","Hebrew"],"rows":[["needs to, must","yes — צְרִיכָה, צְרִיכִים","צָרִיךְ"],["can, is able to","yes — יְכוֹלָה, יְכוֹלִים","יָכוֹל"],["obliged to, must","yes — חַיֶּבֶת, חַיָּבִים","חַיָּב"],["it is possible, one may","no — never changes","אֶפְשָׁר"],["it is permitted","no — never changes","מֻתָּר"],["it is forbidden","no — never changes","אָסוּר"],["it is worth it","no — never changes","כְּדַאי"],["it is hard to","no — never changes","קָשֶׁה"]]},{"type":"text","body":"The split in that last column is the whole grammar of the lesson. צָרִיךְ, יָכוֹל and חַיָּב describe a person, so they agree with that person. אֶפְשָׁר, מֻתָּר, אָסוּר and כְּדַאי describe the situation, so there is nobody for them to agree with and they never move."},{"type":"examples","items":[{"he":"אֲנִי צָרִיךְ לָלֶכֶת.","en":"I have to go."},{"he":"הִיא צְרִיכָה לַעֲבֹד מָחָר.","en":"She has to work tomorrow."},{"he":"אֶפְשָׁר לִשְׁאֹל שְׁאֵלָה?","en":"May I ask a question?"},{"he":"אָסוּר לְעַשֵּׁן כָּאן.","en":"Smoking is forbidden here."},{"he":"כְּדַאי לְךָ לָבוֹא מָחָר.","en":"It's worth your while to come tomorrow."}]},{"type":"tip","body":"To say who a subjectless sentence is about, add לְ with an ending: כְּדַאי לְךָ, אָסוּר לָהֶם, קָשֶׁה לִי. The sentence still has no grammatical subject — the person arrives as an indirect object."},{"type":"tip","body":"The negative of אֶפְשָׁר is not לֹא אֶפְשָׁר but אִי אֶפְשָׁר. That אִי־ is a prefix meaning un-, and it turns up on other words too: אִי־הֲבָנָה, a misunderstanding."}],"vocab":[{"he":"מֻתָּר","en":"it is permitted","translit":"mutar"},{"he":"אָסוּר","en":"it is forbidden","translit":"asur"},{"he":"כְּדַאי","en":"it is worth it","translit":"kedai"},{"he":"חַיָּב","en":"obliged, must","translit":"chayav"},{"he":"אִי אֶפְשָׁר","en":"it is impossible","translit":"i efshar"},{"he":"צְרִיכָה","en":"needs to (f.)","translit":"tsricha"},{"he":"רַשַּׁאי","en":"entitled to, allowed to","translit":"rashai"}],"exercises":[{"type":"choice","q":"What does this mean?","options":["ever since","door","it is permitted","very"],"correct":2,"prompt":"מֻתָּר","translit":"mutar"},{"type":"choice","q":"What does this mean?","options":["it is forbidden","cooks","narrative, legend","beginning"],"correct":0,"prompt":"אָסוּר","translit":"asur"},{"type":"choice","q":"What does this mean?","options":["stomach, belly","helped (someone)","it is worth it","was (f.)"],"correct":2,"prompt":"כְּדַאי","translit":"kedai"},{"type":"choice","q":"What does this mean?","options":["enough","obliged, must","salary","clear"],"correct":1,"prompt":"חַיָּב","translit":"chayav"},{"type":"choice","q":"What does this mean?","options":["Gemara","flower","it is impossible","can, is able"],"correct":2,"prompt":"אִי אֶפְשָׁר","translit":"i efshar"},{"type":"choice","q":"What does this mean?","options":["moon","her","needs to (f.)","two (f.)"],"correct":2,"prompt":"צְרִיכָה","translit":"tsricha"},{"type":"choice","q":"What does this mean?","options":["instead of","sick, a patient","interesting","entitled to, allowed to"],"correct":3,"prompt":"רַשַּׁאי","translit":"rashai"},{"type":"cloze","sentence":"הִיא ___ לַעֲבֹד מָחָר.","en":"She has to work tomorrow.","options":["כְּדַאי","רַשַּׁאי","צְרִיכָה","מֻתָּר"],"correct":2,"full":"הִיא צְרִיכָה לַעֲבֹד מָחָר."},{"type":"choice","q":"The words that carry these sentences — it is hard to?","options":["חַיָּב","צָרִיךְ","קָשֶׁה","יָכוֹל"],"correct":2,"he":true},{"type":"listen","q":"Which word did you hear?","options":["אָסוּר","אִי אֶפְשָׁר","רַשַּׁאי","כְּדַאי"],"correct":0,"say":"אָסוּר","he":true},{"type":"choice","q":"How do you say “it is worth it”?","options":["שֶׁל","יְדַבֵּר","כְּדַאי","בִּלְתִּי"],"correct":2,"he":true},{"type":"choice","q":"How do you say “it is impossible”?","options":["זוֹל","אֹרֶז","הֵיטֵב","אִי אֶפְשָׁר"],"correct":3,"he":true},{"type":"match","pairs":[["מֻתָּר","it is permitted"],["אָסוּר","it is forbidden"],["כְּדַאי","it is worth it"],["חַיָּב","obliged, must"],["אִי אֶפְשָׁר","it is impossible"]]},{"type":"build","tokens":["כָּאן","אָסוּר","לְעַשֵּׁן"],"answer":["אָסוּר","לְעַשֵּׁן","כָּאן"],"en":"Smoking is forbidden here."},{"type":"build","tokens":["צְרִיכָה","מָחָר","לַעֲבֹד","הִיא"],"answer":["הִיא","צְרִיכָה","לַעֲבֹד","מָחָר"],"en":"She has to work tomorrow."},{"type":"type","q":"Write “it is impossible” in Hebrew","answer":"אִי אֶפְשָׁר","hint":"i efshar"}]}